08-21 01:48:36.028 22575 22575 I flutter : [FAIL] endpoint=gateway:wallet-add-fund http_status=null type=ApiFailure transport_error=webview_proxy_authentication msg="payment_webview_load_failed"

08-21 01:51:16.173 22575 22575 I flutter : [FAIL] endpoint=gateway:wallet-add-fund http_status=null type=ApiFailure transport_error=webview_proxy_authentication msg="payment_webview_load_failed"

This live run PROVES the wiring end-to-end: the real flutter_inappwebview
onReceivedError(WebResourceErrorType) callback reaches
PaymentWebViewFailureHelper.handleLoadError, is derived via
`'webview_' + errorType.toValue().toLowerCase()`, and lands in the
console [FAIL] line's transport_error= field exactly as coded.

--- Live trigger for a second DISTINCT value: attempted via a minimal
    CONNECT-tunnel-completing proxy (200 Connection Established then
    silent) to force a genuine origin-side TLS timeout distinct from the
    proxy-negotiation code above. This hung indefinitely (>5 min, no
    onLoadStart/onReceivedError fired) without a bounded non-root way to
    force it -- iptables/adb root are both explicitly disallowed on this
    device pool (NEARS-1069), and Android's WebView proxy vocabulary
    (ERROR_PROXY_AUTHENTICATION) evidently absorbs every unreachable/
    malformed/silent PROXY condition we could produce without root,
    regardless of the underlying cause (DNS-unresolvable proxy host,
    closed proxy port, and a proxy that accepts-but-never-responds all
    produced the identical code). Per the ticket's own escape hatch
    ("fall back to the pinned unit test... state explicitly"), the SECOND
    distinct transport_error= value is demonstrated via the pinned,
    reviewed unit test in this same commit instead:

